course: dictionary
lesson: 11237
title: Workload: the amount of work to do
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Workload is a noun. It means the amount of work a person or group has to do in a certain time. Read this sentence: «When a parent seems frazzled after work, it is usually the workload, not anything the child did.» The clue is «after work»: the parent is tired because of how much work there is.
explain: Frazzled means very tired and stressed, and the sentence says the workload is the reason. So workload must mean the amount of work that wears someone out. Check it by swapping in your meaning: «it is usually the amount of work, not anything the child did». It still makes sense.
explain: A workload can be heavy or light. «A heavy workload» means lots to do; «a manageable workload» means you can cope with it. Words like «share the workload» and «reduce the workload» tell you the work can be divided up or made smaller.
explain: Computers and machines have workloads too. A computer's workload is the amount of work it has to do. The same word works in both places, so think about who or what is doing the work.
fact: Workload means the amount of work a person or group has to do in a certain time.
fact: «A heavy workload» means lots of work to do; «a light workload» means not much.
fact: «Reduce the workload» means make it smaller; «share the workload» means divide it between people.
fact: A computer or machine also has a workload: the amount of work it has to do.
fact: «Frazzled» means very tired and stressed.
exercise: «When a parent seems frazzled after work, it is usually the workload, not anything the child did.» What is the workload here? | A kind of tiredness that comes from sleeping badly | A game the family plays together | *The amount of work the parent has to do | The child's homework
exercise: Mia's teacher says, «Let's reduce the workload.» What does reduce the workload most likely mean? | Make the work heavier | *Make the amount of work smaller | Stop the computers | Add more pages to the books
exercise: Which phrase describes work you can cope with? | a heavy workload | *a manageable workload | a frazzled workload | a workload of stones
test: Which word means the amount of work a computer has to do? | Worksheet | *Workload | Workbook | Workplace
test: «The team shared the workload so nobody felt frazzled.» What does shared the workload mean? | Argued about who was tallest | Gave all the work to one person | *Divided the work between them | Stopped working for the day
exam: Which sentence uses workload correctly? | I ate a big workload of crisps | *Our class has a heavy workload this week | My workload is my favourite colour | The workload of the sky is blue
